5b545161 (2214 1792744525713 "guenchi" (("w5vbequy" . 14982)) (put ((kind . section) (title . "landing f322") (src . "drafts: a draft whose base fell behind says so, and the freeze brings every draft onto the applied cut\n\nA landing's drafts were frozen at the cut they were made at, and a committed\nrecord by any writer after that (a review letter is one) moved the applied\ncut, so landing-commit refused the freeze as cut-moved and every draft had to\nbe rebased by hand. `drafts --writer W` now answers, after its items, one\n`(behind <block> (based-on <hash>) (committed <hash>))` per draft whose base is\nno longer the block's committed version, computed from the same comparison as\nits `(now ..) (fresh ..)` clauses. tools/landing-freeze.sc brings every draft\nonto the applied cut before it dumps: a draft whose block did not change is\nmoved (`--rebase` at the same version), a draft whose block changed is merged\nby a line-based three-way merge (base at the draft's cut, ours, the committed\ntext; the four alignments must agree, and overlapping or abutting edits are a\nconflict) or the freeze stops with `drafts-behind-conflict` naming every\nconflicting block; the freeze record carries `(rebased ..)` and `(moved ..)`\nlines and the pinned cut is the applied cut; a commit during the freeze is\nrefused cut-moved. `landing-freeze.sc <store> --writer W --check <dir>`\nvalidates a frozen directory (every item's sha256 and version, a one-datum\nmanifest) and answers current, refreeze (naming behind and moved blocks) or\nthe conflict refusal, so a landing script can stop before its start tag.\ntest/drafts-tools.sc grows the rows; the README's drafts paragraph says who\nmoves the applied cut and what `behind` means.\n") (parent . root) (ord . 455))))
